2. The combineByKey() solution
========================================================================
(orders, total, first order, last order, favorite product)
alice (4, 63.0, '2026-01-05', '2026-03-15', 'coffee')
bob (3, 21.0, '2026-01-07', '2026-03-02', 'tea')
carol (1, 30.0, '2026-02-28', '2026-02-28', 'teapot')

We miss you! list (no order since 2026-03-01): ['carol']

========================================================================
3. Can't we just use map() + reduceByKey()?
========================================================================
first one-order profile: ('alice', {'orders': 1, 'total': 12.0, 'first': '2026-01-05', 'last': '2026-01-05', 'products': {'coffee': 1}})
mapValues(start_profile).reduceByKey(merge_profiles) same answer? True

Same answer, but rule 2 ('add an order') disappears: 8 fake
'profiles' for 3 customers, and one profile dictionary built for
EVERY order. combineByKey() builds one profile per customer per
partition, and the code reads like the business rules.

========================================================================
4. And aggregateByKey()?
========================================================================
fake empty profile: {'orders': 0, 'total': 0.0, 'first': '9999-12-31', 'last': '0000-01-01', 'products': {}}
aggregateByKey(zero, add_order, merge_profiles)      same answer? True

It works, but a profile with 0 orders and a first order in the year
9999 makes no sense in the business. With combineByKey(), the
customer's first REAL order starts the profile.
